WEBVTT

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.280
The genius from the 
darkness, Akagi Shigeru,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.790
and the monster of the 
Showa Era, Washizu Iwao.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.290
Akagi's wager is a fatal amount of blood.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.290
Washizu's is the entirety 
of his remaining fortune.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.390
A deathmatch with the aim to 
completely annihilate the opponent.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.000
South Hand 1.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.800
Akagi outsmarts Washizu and draws closer.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.300
Pretty good.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.080
Any other kind of play would be boring.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.510
<i>Come back at me with 
everything you've got.</i>

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.080
<i>Show me that very last 
shred of resistance</i>

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.360
<i>when I've got you backed into the corner.</i>

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:56.880
<i>That, for me, is the ultimate pleasure!</i>

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.440
<b>Episode 18</b>

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.440
<b>The Cursed Tiles</b>

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.090
South Hand 2.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.450
Washizu is the dealer.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.470
Washizu currently has 37,400 points.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.570
Akagi has 28,600 points.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.570
The difference has shrunk to 8,800.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.010
<i>You probably think you 
got a good blow in,</i>

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.580
<i>but that doesn't change the fact 
that I'm still ahead of you.</i>

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.180
That is correct.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.320
It might have shrunk, but the 
difference is still 8,800 points.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.630
Unless Akagi can overturn 
this difference before

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.830
this East-South match ends, 
he will not survive.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.230
<i>If he loses by rank,</i>

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.930
<i>he'll lose over 2,000 CCs the 
moment the East-South match ends.</i>

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.930
<i>Akagi will not survive.</i>

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:42.640
<i>He's in danger.</i>

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.360
<i>The fact that he's in an overwhelmingly
dangerous situation hasn't changed.</i>

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:46.940
Pon.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:48.940
<i>And yet...</i>

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:54.390
<i>How can he be so calm?</i>

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.580
<i>He's on the verge of dying.</i>

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.590
<i>He'll die if he doesn't get back on top.</i>

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.690
<i>That's the situation he's in, and yet...</i>

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.020
It goes without saying 
that this South Hand 2

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.690
with Washizu as the dealer 
is very important.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.230
If he is able to safely 
work his way to a win,

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.660
winning the entire East-South 
match becomes a possibility.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:14.730
<i>But if Washizu is the one who wins, 
Akagi's win from the</i>

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.400
<i>previous hand will essentially be void.</i>

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.100
<i>The difference will exceed 10,000 again, 
and Washizu will still be the dealer.</i>

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.410
<i>That's something that needs 
to be avoided at all costs.</i>

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.780
<i>He has to bump Washizu from his 
dealer seat, no matter the cost.</i>

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.240
<i>I can't imagine that he wouldn't know 
how crucial that will be for this match.</i>

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:33.790
<i>And yet...</i>

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.290
<i>How can he remain so calm?</i>

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.890
It's almost as if his 
indifference attracted it,

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.650
as Akagi draws the key to 
his hand, the red dragon.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.230
With the transparent tiles that 
are used in Washizu Mahjong,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.140
calling a red dragon from an 
opponent is essentially impossible.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.510
If he wanted to call it, he'd have 
to call it from his partner, Yasuoka.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.740
On the flip side, it's also extremely 
hard to get rid of these tiles.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.710
<i>And he already got a triplet 
of them this early in the game.</i>

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.580
The wind was starting to pick up.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:12.220
The tailwind that had faded 
before had returned once more.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:17.050
<i>He's got two dora as his pair, 
and is one away from tenpai.</i>

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:20.800
<i>But it looks like Washizu's 
hand is quite fast, too.</i>

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.940
<i>And the underling to his right 
is also getting ready to deal</i>

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.640
<i>into Washizu's hand right away.</i>

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:31.660
<i>Who will it be?</i>

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.140
<i>Who will be in tenpai first?</i>

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.140
<i>Akagi?</i>

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:37.650
<i>Or Washizu?</i>

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.290
<i>All right!</i>

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.230
<i>Akagi got there first.</i>

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:43.650
<i>He has a 5 or 8 of circles wait.</i>

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.750
This is what Akagi's hand 
looks like to Washizu.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.190
It's almost certain that Akagi's waits

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.400
are for the 5, 8 of circles, 
or 2, 5 of bamboo.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.480
<i>None of his opponents will 
ever deal into his hand.</i>

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.130
<i>He can only count on his 
own draws or Yasuoka.</i>

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:05.110
However, Yasuoka does not have 
either of those tiles in his hand.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:06.840
<i>Perfect!</i>

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:09.580
<i>There it is!</i>

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.770
A priceless draw!

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.280
The tile that can bump

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.910
Washizu out of his dealer seat, 
and give Akagi a mangan hand.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.820
The 8 of circles is a 
veritable deus ex machina!

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:22.810
<i>What?!</i>

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.020
<i>Why doesn't he take it?</i>

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.170
<i>What are you thinking, Akagi?!</i>

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.020
At this moment, Ougi is too 
fixated on winning to see it.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.000
The trap that is built into Akagi's hand...

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.710
The curse that Akagi's tiles cast 
on Washizu, binding him in place.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:43.370
That's right.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:46.200
Akagi's wait can either be a 5, 
8 of circles, or 2, 5 of bamboo.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.980
That's what the tiles reveal.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.220
If one of the options, 
the 8 of circles, passes,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.110
it becomes likely that Akagi's actually 
waiting for the 2, 5 of bamboo.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.720
It's almost certain.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.250
<i>I see.</i>

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:03.050
<i>This 2, 5 of bamboo wait is nothing 
but an illusion for Washizu.</i>

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.630
But this illusion actually binds him.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.140
At this time, Washizu is also in tenpai.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.380
If he can discard either 
the 2 or 5 of bamboo,

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.640
his underling can deal 
into his hand right away.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:17.640
But he can't take the necessary step.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.880
Those are not tiles he can discard.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.880
He's forced to be defensive.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.380
<i>An absolute and decisive way 
of slowing your opponent down</i>

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.950
<i>that's only possible with these glass tiles.</i>

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:34.490
<i>So Akagi didn't have to rush to win, 
he still had time to spare.</i>

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:36.590
Then, on the 10th turn...

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.000
His patience is rewarded.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:43.230
Red dragon, west wind, two dora. Mangan.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:45.760
He draws it himself.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.470
The mangan causes the upset!

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.290
If he had taken Yasuoka's tile, 
it wouldn't have caused an upset.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:52.710
But if he draws it himself,

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.710
the 4,000 coming from Washizu 
are enough to tip the scales.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:00.950
Akagi is now the one on top 
again, with a lead of 3,200.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:05.300
He was able to draw the 8 of circles 
himself by ignoring Yasuoka's.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.430
<i>You bastard, you'll pay for that!</i>

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.030
<i>He can overcome it...</i>

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.400
<i>If he survives two more hands, 
he can overcome this East-South match.</i>

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.030
<i>At that point, the rules say that 
the intermediate payouts are done,</i>

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:25.410
<i>so he can get back the blood he lost.</i>

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.910
<i>The crazy rate of 20,000 points 
for 2,000 CCs of blood...</i>

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.520
<i>It's not impossible for Akagi.</i>

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.520
<i>He just has to repeat this six times.</i>

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.910
<i>He might not take all of Washizu's 
fortune, the entire 500 million,</i>

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:43.760
<i>but he might be able to take 200</i>

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.810
<i>or 300 million and leave 
this mansion alive.</i>

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:48.890
<i>He can do it.</i>

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:53.240
<i>It would be impossible for others, 
but you can do it, Akagi!</i>

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:55.600
South Hand 3.

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.730
Washizu's underling, Suzuki, is the dealer.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:03.010
The last two hands on 
the edge of death begin,

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.850
both parties with daggers at their throats.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.850
<i>I see, he's got some skill.</i>

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.090
<i>He's not just some trash.</i>

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:21.100
<i>He's not a common idiot who rushes 
blindly to his own death, either.</i>

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.490
<i>He might seem like one, but he isn't.</i>

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:26.820
<i>This man is able to throw 
away his own advantage.</i>

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:30.180
<i>An ordinary daredevil isn't 
capable of doing that.</i>

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:36.080
<i>They might be able to ignore 
disadvantages and rush in blindly,</i>

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.650
<i>but they can't throw away tangible 
advantages once they have them.</i>

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:43.320
<i>Advantages are just that tempting.</i>

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:47.820
<i>But this man can throw 
away those advantages.</i>

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.060
<i>Without any hesitation, too.</i>

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.450
<i>Normal predictions won't work with him.</i>

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.900
<i>He's obviously different 
from the ordinary losers</i>

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.070
<i>who acted all tough before I killed them.</i>

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:05.300
<i>But sometimes, 
even talent is meaningless.</i>

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.540
<i>That's the essence of Mahjong.</i>

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:09.250
<i>Die!</i>

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:13.070
<i>This is interesting.</i>

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.550
<i>I could take this to a multi-sided wait,</i>

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:20.030
<i>but it looks like that 1 of circles 
in Akagi's hand might come out.</i>

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.290
<i>If he goes into tenpai, 
he'll discard it.</i>

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:26.790
<i>Until then, it'll be fun to do a 
single wait on this 1 of circles.</i>

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:32.470
Akagi is in tenpai.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.270
By discarding the 1 of circles, he'll have a 
multi-sided wait for 1, 4, 7 of characters.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:36.830
<i>What are you going to do?</i>

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:38.510
<i>If you can make the 1 of circles pass,</i>

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.110
<i>Yasuoka can immediately deal the 
4 of characters into your hand.</i>

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:46.590
<i>But it's entirely possible that 
Washizu is waiting for that very tile.</i>

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:49.510
<i>Of course, he could also have 
the 1 of circles as his pair</i>

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:51.430
<i>and have a closed wait 
for the 4 of circles,</i>

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.850
<i>or a double-sided wait 
for 3, 6 of circles.</i>

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.330
<i>Alternatively, he could 
also not be in tenpai yet.</i>

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:05.200
<i>Should he take his chances here and 
discard the tile we all know is risky?</i>

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:08.410
<i>He went around it.</i>

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.700
<i>He's playing it safe.</i>

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.300
<i>That's right.</i>

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:14.340
<i>That's the kind of man you are.</i>

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.070
<i>You will see through this kind of ruse.</i>

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.310
<i>But don't forget...</i>

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.780
<i>Even if you see what's going on, 
still losing is what Mahjong is all about.</i>

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.030
Riichi!

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.670
Washizu changes his wait.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:32.260
He drew the perfect tile, 
the 6 of circles.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:37.130
He now has the ideal multi-sided 
wait for 2, 5, 8 or 3, 6 of circles.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.880
As Akagi had to break up his hand 
to avoid discarding the 1 of circles,

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:42.500
he's now one tile away from tenpai.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:46.390
<i>Do you get it now?</i>

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.840
<i>To have the right instincts and courage</i>

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.940
<i>to play perfectly and still lose 
is what Mahjong is all about.</i>

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:56.070
<i>It's only natural.</i>

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:59.350
<i>We're in completely 
different leagues anyway.</i>

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:04.090
<i>By using tricks, you were finally 
able to approach my realm.</i>

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:09.690
<i>But I don't need any of that to stop you...</i>

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:14.930
<i>And win.</i>

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.150
I didn't get any reverse dora, 
but it's a riichi,

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:21.450
ippatsu, tsumo, south wind, 
one dora hand. Mangan.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:22.540
Looks like I overtook you.

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:27.040
Only one hand left.

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:32.980
I've got you cornered, Akagi!

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:38.370
Because Washizu got a tsumo,

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.490
Akagi has to pay 2,000 points 
and 200 CCs of blood.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.860
In total, Akagi has lost 
600 CCs of blood so far,

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.100
a third of the fatal amount.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.030
After this upset, 
there's only one hand left.

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:55.470
If Akagi doesn't win South Hand 4, 
he'll lose the entire game.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.490
If he loses by rank, 
including the ranking bonus,

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:00.770
there'll be a difference of 
over 50,000 points to Washizu.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:04.840
That corresponds to 5,000 CCs of blood.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.810
Instant death.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.920
His life is literally riding on this one hand.

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:14.560
<i>So far, Akagi made it this 
far by managing to avoid</i>

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.660
<i>dealing into Washizu's hand.</i>

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.160
<i>The defensive play in East Hand 1.</i>

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:21.630
<i>The quick win in East Hand 2.</i>

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.760
<i>Waiting for the tile that 
he knew would flow out</i>

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:26.170
<i>of Washizu's hand in South Hand 1.</i>

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:29.280
<i>Basically, he neatly 
avoids traps and attacks</i>

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.040
<i>to score wins whenever 
Washizu has an opening.</i>

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.890
This is no ordinary game.

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:39.550
He is constantly aware of Washizu 
and plays accordingly with restraint.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.900
But occasionally, playing Mahjong like that

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.270
will distort the flow of the game.

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:48.690
Like rotten wood gathers in the 
stagnant part of the river,

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.840
Akagi's hand only has tiles that 
seem to have no coherence at all.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:58.260
On the other hand, 
Washizu gets an easy hand.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:00.630
A starting hand with a clear direction.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:02.000
Unconstrained draws.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.130
It's not just a matter 
of the point difference.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.370
Akagi is suffering.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:11.480
In this upstream battle, 
Akagi is the dealer for the last hand.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:15.210
This is Akagi's starting hand.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.160
<i>I can't say it's completely terrible...</i>

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:20.490
<i>but it's a pretty harsh starting hand.</i>

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.910
<i>Luckily, he has pairs of honor tiles</i>

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:25.960
<i>and Yasuoka has both the 
east wind and the red dragon.</i>

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.500
<i>He could call them any time he wants.</i>

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.550
<i>So in reality, Akagi already 
has half his hand complete.</i>

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.010
But with only the east 
wind and the red dragon,

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:38.300
he'd only get as far as 
3,900 or 4,800 points.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.310
It won't be enough to close 
the gap of 6,800 to Washizu.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:45.940
<i>He'll have to get a direct 
hit to cause an upset.</i>

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:49.140
<i>But I can't imagine 
that Washizu would make</i>

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:51.000
<i>such a mistake in this last hand.</i>

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:51.970
<i>So then...</i>

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.990
<i>He has to go for a 
honitsu or a toitoi hand.</i>

00:13:55.990 --> 00:14:00.460
<i>Or draw a dora and incorporate 
it into his hand somehow...</i>

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.330
<i>Either way, he can't afford 
to drag his feet for long.</i>

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.700
<i>From what I can see, 
Washizu's got a good hand.</i>

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:11.550
<i>He's probably two or three tiles from tenpai.</i>

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.430
<i>On top of that, his underling 
has the 5 of characters</i>

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:17.570
<i>and 3 of circles that that will, 
very likely, be Washizu's winning tiles.</i>

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:21.650
<i>How could he draw that tile?</i>

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:23.080
<i>Not only did he advance his hand with that,</i>

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:25.080
<i>he's also managed to turn the</i>

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:27.450
<i>problematic green dragons 
into a triplet already!</i>

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.320
<i>Now it's sure that he's two 
tiles away from tenpai.</i>

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:33.730
<i>It would be ideal for Akagi to get 
one tile away from tenpai by himself</i>

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:36.830
<i>so I can give him the east 
wind, red dragon combo...</i>

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.980
<i>But we don't have much time.</i>

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.060
<i>Right now, one extra turn could be fatal.</i>

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:44.300
<i>Akagi, call it!</i>

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:50.000
<i>You're going to wait?</i>

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.250
<i>We don't have any time to waste here!</i>

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.080
<i>He got another good tile.</i>

00:14:58.550 --> 00:14:59.880
<i>One away from tenpai?</i>

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:02.750
Fifth turn, Yasuoka.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:06.570
<i>Akagi has a pair of these.</i>

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:08.960
<i>Now I can give him three tiles.</i>

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:10.570
<i>Great!</i>

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:11.950
<i>We can't wait any longer!</i>

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:13.960
<i>Do it, Akagi! Call them!</i>

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:20.040
<i>Not yet?!</i>

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:21.700
<i>You want me to wait longer?!</i>

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:27.210
<i>Akagi, what are you...</i>

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:29.490
<i>What are you thinking?</i>

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.890
<i>How can you discard that?</i>

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:44.190
<i>Even if that's a dora, it'll be 
completely isolated in your hand.</i>

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:46.430
The next turn, Akagi...

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:51.660
<i>Is this... a chiitoitsu?</i>

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:54.170
<i>Is he going for a chiitoitsu?</i>

00:15:55.160 --> 00:16:00.020
As the dealer, with the two dora, 
Akagi would get 9,600 for his chiitoitsu.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:03.580
Even if Yasuoka deals into his hand, 
it would be enough to cause an upset.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:08.720
Also, this is what his hand 
looks like to Washizu.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.080
He could easily assume Akagi's in tenpai.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.880
<i>If it goes well, this could 
force Washizu to slow down.</i>

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:15.820
<i>But...</i>

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:18.620
<i>You fool.</i>

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:19.790
<i>Are you trying to tie me down?</i>

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:24.530
<i>I guess you're trying to pretend 
you're in tenpai with a chiitoitsu hand,</i>

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:27.270
<i>but that's absolutely impossible.</i>

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:32.490
<i>In order for that to be true, two 
of the three black tiles you have</i>

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:35.770
<i>must match the single 7 of 
characters and 4 of bamboo.</i>

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.560
<i>But that's already not possible.</i>

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:45.280
<i>It's impossible... because I 
already have a black 4 of bamboo.</i>

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:49.020
<i>His chiitoitsu is one tile 
away from tenpai, at best.</i>

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:52.960
<i>You have no luck,</i>

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:57.970
<i>no matter how much you struggle 
or scream in this last hand.</i>

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:01.130
<i>If I didn't know where the 
black 4 of bamboo was,</i>

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:05.510
<i>your fake chiitoitsu might have 
been somewhat of a threat.</i>

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.250
<i>Not only didn't it turn out that way,</i>

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:09.740
<i>but your hand isn't progressing, either.</i>

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:12.320
<i>That's obvious, though.</i>

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:17.750
<i>If your hand was ready, 
you'd have no reason to hesitate</i>

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:19.980
<i>since your life's riding on this one hand.</i>

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:22.840
<i>You'd take your partner's east wind and win</i>

00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:25.090
<i>with the red dragon he's got ready for you.</i>

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:28.800
<i>With that one dora, you as 
the dealer would get 5,800</i>

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.460
<i>for that 30 fu, 3 han hand.</i>

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:33.260
<i>You wouldn't cause an upset,</i>

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.200
<i>but you'd survive to continue the game.</i>

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:38.950
<i>If I win this hand, it's all over for you.</i>

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:40.440
<i>You're at the end of the line.</i>

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:42.630
<i>You can't be so naïve as to think that</i>

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:45.630
<i>not being able to overtake 
me is a good enough reason.</i>

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:49.360
<i>Once I'm in tenpai, it's over!</i>

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:57.200
<i>If you could have made your move, 
you would have done so already!</i>

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.360
<i>Since you haven't...</i>

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:02.180
<i>You're still not in tenpai,
even after calling</i>

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:03.790
<i>the east wind, red dragon, 
and 1 of characters?</i>

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.890
<i>That just goes to show 
how bad your hand is.</i>

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:11.270
<i>No matter how you look at it, 
I can get my last tile to tenpai faster.</i>

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:14.250
<i>On top of that, I can 
choose to go for either</i>

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:17.000
<i>the 5 of characters wait or 
the 3, 6 of circles wait.</i>

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:21.080
<i>Suzuki will be able to deal 
into my hand either way.</i>

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.240
<i>Everything is taken care of.</i>

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.790
<i>Akagi, you will lose.</i>

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.490
<i>Your downfall was to think your little tricks</i>

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:31.990
<i>would affect the flow of the game.</i>

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.720
<i>You can't recover from that mistake now.</i>

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:39.230
<i>Now you'll die.</i>

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:42.280
<i>Suzuki also has the 5 of bamboo.</i>

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:46.470
<i>That means he can deal into the 
2, 5, 8 of bamboo wait, as well.</i>

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.840
<i>Then I don't need this closed wait here.</i>

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:52.980
<i>Now my hand is even stronger.</i>

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.340
<i>The game is going in my favor!</i>

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:58.440
This one move...

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:00.800
This was the only time in this last hand

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:02.820
where Washizu showed 
any sign of relaxing.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:07.140
<i>So you're going to call now?</i>

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:13.030
Pon.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:14.760
The opportunity arrives.

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:15.490
Pon.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:18.340
Pon.

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:22.970
Akagi rapidly calls three times.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:27.170
He's one away from tenpai.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.920
However, to Washizu, 
his hand looks like this.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:34.490
<i>I get it!</i>

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:35.950
<i>This was Akagi's plan.</i>

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:37.580
<i>The reason he held back</i>

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:40.590
<i>on calling these three tiles 
was to create this opportunity.</i>

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.140
<i>There are two reasons.</i>

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:44.660
<i>First, even if he couldn't get into tenpai,</i>

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:47.120
<i>he waited for his hand to 
fill up with character tiles.</i>

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:53.140
<i>In other words, he waited until his hand 
would look like he's in tenpai to Washizu.</i>

00:19:54.790 --> 00:19:56.560
And the other,

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:59.220
very important reason is 
this 4 of characters.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:01.810
If Akagi had called these three tiles

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:05.790
and hinted at a honitsu hand 
before Washizu had discarded it,

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:10.180
Washizu simply would have kept his 
4 and 6 of characters in his hand.

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:13.280
Already in good shape, 
Washizu would not have slowed down.

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.890
But once he broke up the closed wait by

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:18.790
discarding the 4 of characters, 
the situation completely changed.

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:23.790
Once the leftover 6 of characters is 
a risky tile, things are not the same.

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:27.680
<i>Only now can he actually 
force Washizu to stop.</i>

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:29.950
<i>This is bad for him.</i>

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:33.240
<i>Now he has to wait for 
something that lets him</i>

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.270
<i>incorporate that 6 of 
characters into his hand.</i>

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:41.230
<i>This is precisely the reason 
that Akagi laid low and waited.</i>

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:43.780
<i>He waited until Washizu 
relaxed a tiny bit.</i>

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:47.030
<i>Perfect calls.</i>

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.520
<i>He put everything into this bluff.</i>

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:53.260
<i>Now we'll have to see if this 
will actually work on Washizu.</i>

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:56.490
<i>You brat...</i>

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:59.160
The last hand on the verge of death...

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.920
The deathmatch of destruction 
continues down the dark tunnel,

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.320
and sets an even larger 
wave into motion.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:19.140
Next time, 
"The Confusion of the Demon."

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next episode</b>

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>The Confusion of the Demon</b>
